  <p>
    Agouti signaling protein — ASIP — is the reason the melanocortin pigment system has
    an off switch as well as an on switch. Where <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> activates{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> to make dark pigment, ASIP does the opposite: it
    is the natural blocker that shifts colour the other way. It is the original "agouti"
    molecule, it gives its name to a whole family of melanocortin antagonists, and it
    carries one of the most instructive lessons in the system — that a brake is only
    harmless where it belongs.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="A brake in its place, and a brake everywhere. Released locally in the hair follicle, ASIP blocks MC1R and writes a pale band into a dark hair. Expressed in every tissue — the lethal-yellow mouse, the human ASIP duplication — the same protein also reaches MC4R in the hypothalamus, and the result is a yellow or red coat plus obesity, overgrowth and high insulin."
    source="Schematic, after Bultman et al. 1992, Lu et al. 1994, Fan et al. 1997 and Kempf et al. 2022."
  >
    <BrakeInItsPlace />
  </Figure>

  <h2>The gene behind a hundred years of mouse genetics</h2>
  <p>
    The agouti locus was one of the first genes in mammalian genetics, mapped by its
    effect on mouse coat colour decades before anyone knew what it encoded. In 1992 and
    1993 two groups cloned it and found a gene for a small secreted protein of 131 amino
    acids, expressed in the hair follicle and epidermis exactly when yellow pigment is
    being laid down.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={2} /> The same work explained a mystery that
    had puzzled geneticists since the 1920s. The dominant <em>lethal yellow</em> allele
    makes mice uniformly yellow, obese, diabetic and tumour-prone; the cloning showed
    that it is a chromosomal rearrangement that splices a ubiquitous promoter onto the
    intact agouti coding sequence, so that a protein meant for the hair follicle is made
    in nearly every tissue of the body.<Cite n={2} /> The pigment and the obesity, it
    turned out, were the same molecule in two places.
  </p>

  <h2>Type-switching the pigment</h2>
  <p>
    Pigment cells can make two kinds of melanin: dark, UV-shielding <strong>eumelanin</strong>
    when MC1R is active, and light red-yellow <strong>pheomelanin</strong> when it is
    not. In 1994 agouti protein was shown to be what its genetics predicted: a
    high-affinity antagonist of the MSH receptor that blocks α-MSH from raising cyclic
    AMP, the signal through which the receptor drives eumelanin
    synthesis.<Cite n={3} /> In human melanocytes it does more than compete: pretreating
    the cells with ASIP leaves them unable to respond to α-MSH at all, which looks like
    receptor desensitisation rather than simple blockade, and it lowers the receptor's
    basal activity as an inverse agonist.<Cite n={7} /> The result is "pigment
    type-switching": the same cell, the same receptor, a different output depending on
    whether the accelerator (α-MSH) or the brake (ASIP) has the upper hand. A pulse of
    ASIP while a hair is growing produces the pale sub-terminal band of the classic
    agouti hair — a brief lift of the accelerator written into the fibre as it
    forms.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>What the protein looks like</h2>
  <p>
    ASIP's business end is its cysteine-rich C-terminal domain, whose NMR structure was
    solved in 2005. It folds as an <strong>inhibitor cystine knot</strong>, a tight,
    disulfide-braced scaffold otherwise known from spider and cone-snail toxins; ASIP
    and its cousin AgRP are the only mammalian proteins in the class.<Cite n={6} /> The
    structure also held a surprise: the domain flips between two conformations around a
    pair of prolines, and only the minor one, with two unusual <em>cis</em> peptide
    bonds, is active at the melanocortin receptors.<Cite n={6} /> The N-terminal half of
    the protein has a different job. It is needed for ASIP to interact with
    <strong>attractin</strong>, a large membrane protein that acts as a low-affinity
    co-receptor: mice lacking attractin are immune to the yellow coat and the obesity of
    the lethal-yellow allele, because the agouti protein can no longer act on the cells
    it reaches.<Cite n={5} /> AgRP, by contrast, does not need attractin — one of the
    ways the two cousins have gone their separate ways.
  </p>

  <h2>Cousin to the hunger peptide</h2>
  <p>
    ASIP's trick — block a melanocortin receptor — was borrowed once more by the body,
    in the brain. <a href="/agrp">AgRP</a> (agouti-related peptide) is its cousin: it
    blocks <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> and <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a> to
    drive appetite, exactly as ASIP blocks MC1R to lighten pigment. Same fold, same
    mechanism, different tissue, different job — a neat example of evolution reusing a
    working idea. It also explains the lethal-yellow mouse. Agouti protein antagonises
    MC4R as well as MC1R,<Cite n={3} /> and when it is made throughout the body it
    reaches the hypothalamus and does AgRP's job without AgRP's restraint. The 1997
    experiment that nailed this down gave mice a synthetic melanocortin agonist and
    antagonist into the brain: the agonist suppressed feeding even in lethal-yellow
    mice, the antagonist blocked that and increased feeding on its own, showing that
    melanocortin neurons tonically hold appetite down and that the agouti obesity
    syndrome is what happens when that tone is chronically blocked.<Cite n={4} /> It was
    the experiment that put MC4R at the centre of body-weight biology, and ASIP was the
    molecule that pointed to it.
  </p>

  <h2>The same disease in people</h2>
  <p>
    For thirty years the lethal-yellow mouse looked like a laboratory curiosity. In 2022
    it became a human diagnosis. A girl with extreme early-onset obesity was found to
    carry a tandem duplication at the ASIP locus that places the gene under the control
    of a ubiquitously active promoter — the human equivalent of the mouse
    rearrangement — so that ASIP was made in every cell type examined, including
    hypothalamus-like neurons derived from her own stem cells. Her phenotype matched the
    mouse's point for point: early obesity, overgrowth, red hair and high insulin. Because
    this kind of mutation escapes standard genetic screens, the authors went back through
    a cohort of 1,745 children with obesity and found four more with the identical
    duplication and the same picture.<Cite n={12} /> It is a monogenic cause of human
    obesity that no gene panel was looking for, and it rests on the oldest finding in
    melanocortin genetics: a brake expressed where it does not belong.
  </p>

  <h2>ASIP in human hair, skin and cancer risk</h2>
  <p>
    Humans do not have banded hairs, but ASIP still shapes how we look. A common variant
    in the gene's 3′-untranslated region is associated with dark hair and brown
    eyes,<Cite n={9} /> and a haplotype near ASIP, tagged by two single-nucleotide
    changes, is associated with red hair, freckling and skin that burns rather than tans
    — a spectrum of effects strikingly similar to the red-hair variants of MC1R
    itself.<Cite n={10} /> The same haplotype matters beyond appearance. In more than
    40,000 Europeans it raised the risk of cutaneous melanoma by about 45% and of basal
    cell carcinoma by about a third, and the association held after adjusting for
    pigmentation, which suggests the brake on MC1R affects more than the colour of the
    skin it protects.<Cite n={11} /> The <a href="/genetics">genetics</a> page puts these
    variants alongside MC1R's own.
  </p>

  <h2>Not the only brake</h2>
  <p>
    ASIP is the classic MC1R antagonist but not the only natural one. Dog breeders had
    long known a third coat-colour locus that overrides agouti to give a dominant black
    coat; in 2007 it was identified as a <strong>β-defensin</strong>, a protein family
    previously known only from innate immunity, whose product binds MC1R with high
    affinity and produces black fur in dogs and in transgenic mice.<Cite n={8} /> Human
    β-defensin 3 antagonises MC1R in cultured human melanocytes in the same way ASIP
    does.<Cite n={7} /> The melanocortin receptors, it turns out, have more than one
    family of ligands — and more than one way of being told to stop.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    ASIP completes the pigment half of the melanocortin story. You cannot fully
    understand MC1R, tanning, or the drugs that push pigment (like the tanning peptides)
    without the molecule that pushes the other way. Every melanocortin signal, it turns
    out, comes with a built-in counterweight — and in the skin, ASIP is it. Its deeper
    lesson is about place: the same protein that paints a band on a hair will, released
    everywhere, make a mouse or a child obese. For the receptor it governs, see{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R — the pigment switch</a>; for the cousin that does
    the same job in the brain, see <a href="/agrp">AgRP</a>.
  </p>

